ly fine  however I don't think it's fair to say  all of what I listed before that was cut  had no value I think that's ridiculous  it enriches the world with character and  personality and genuinely without all of  that it feels significantly more barren  and  but mainly what this all has taught me  is that most people hate to wait a lot  of people don't care about what happens  leading up they just want to see the big  moments in a day and age where youtubers  upload multiple long videos every single  day 365 days a year  where Netflix uploads entire seasons of  TV shows to binge from start to finish  the idea of having to delay  gratification for the sake of something  like story is what many fans today's  struggle with which to me is a shame  because I think part of what makes a  journey fund is the journey not the  destination  to prove my point let's take a look at  three of the most popular scenes from  the original dragon ball story to see  how waiting played an important role in  their success  Gamboa  doc ascetically when we the audience  were first introduced to the Prince of  all Saiyans instantly Vegeta became the  symbol of evil in Dragon Ball it wasn't  until we saw how resourceful and nuanced  his character was did we begin to warm  up to him and find him intensely  compelling we watched him suffer and  grow make mistakes and learn because of  this he became one of if not the most  popular character in the entire series  culminating with this scene having been  raised to believe a false set of  ideologies Vegeta's path to redemption  was wrought with turmoil sacrifice and  suffering it was a long journey the  longest character arc in the entire  series by a wide margin  it was the consequences of experiences  and choices Vegeta made that acted as  the building blocks to his character  arcs climax do you think the character  of Vegeta would have been nearly as  compelling and well-received if his  entire story had been condensed into a  single arc due to King Piccolo's actions  over the course of an entire arc he  became known as a malicious and  reprehensible villain as the arc came to  a close we were introduced to piccolo  jr. later referred to simply as piccolo  what initially made this character  interesting was who he was associated  with and because of his association he  then filled the role of Goku's nemesis  in the closing arc of Dragon Ball  however as time moved forward natural  and organic circumstances arose forcing  piccolo to make certain important  choices the consequences of these  choices ultimately led him to share with  us the audience a much-needed catharsis  for his character what made piccolo  compelling as a character were the  circumstances that preceded him the evil  doctrine he was born into and ultimately  the compromises he needed to make over  the course of his entire lifetime in  order to become the person he was  supposed to be this could not have been  told over the course of a small number  of episodes or even an arc it needed  time Gohan or little Mata money shove it  to [ __ ] allah almighty and lastly this  scene  [Music]  this from what I've been exposed to is  widely considered the favorite isolated  moment among fans from the entire series  however as I'm certain you are all aware  of it started here Gohan is a fantastic  example of someone who physically and  emotionally benefited from enough time  passing over a significant portion of  his life Gohan's experiences led him  down a path where he realized he was  worth much more than he ever thought  possible  whether it be against Raddatz nappa  Vegeta his adventures on Namek or even  Frieza Donna throughout Gohan story are  these pockets of performance and rage he  is more than just that of course but  pertaining to that quote great scene in  question hypothetically if you were to  view that clip in isolation without any  backstory it might look cool but it  would not be memorable or impactful the  same way because you didn't experience  the story alongside the character in  other words you never experienced his  struggle - you wouldn't be able to  empathize the point I'm trying to make  with all of these examples is that  despite Dragonball being remembered for  these moments despite the overwhelming  consensus being that Dragonball is great  because of flashy transformations and  big set pieces in reality in actuality  it's great because of the careful  planning and time that went into each  and every one of these characters to  show what I'm talking about doesn't just  relate or pertain to Dragonball  exclusively but to every story medium  let's take a look at a company that  makes a living off of promoting fight  scenes and creating these monumental  moments that need to be great World  Wrestling Entertainment's  [Music]  there's a term in wrestling called  booking this is referring to the means  by which the characters on the show are  scheduled and written to win or lose  otherwise known as their storyline it is  widely accepted within wrestling circles  that long-term booking is far more  favorable than short-term booking this  is essentially comparing long term  storytelling or stories told over a long  period of time to short-term  flash-in-the-pan stories WWE has  hundreds if not thousands of matches  every year but the biggest event on the  WWE calendar is WrestleMania in mid to  late spring WWE holds the event that  acts as the climax to a year's worth of  storytelling often times it acts as the  conclusion to many stories the big  showdown in a show like WrestleMania you  can't just have a main event with no  long term storytelling or booking and if  you do it just wouldn't work there needs  to be a compelling story to draw people  in the characters need to have a  motivation and a motivation comes from a  what their four characters need to want  something in order to fight and you can  communicate that over time through story  to your audience Vegeta wants revenge  against Frieza for the death of his  people  Goku wants to test himself against  strong opponents piccolo wants to  protect his family and Gohan wants to  keep his friends safe one example of  what I'm talking about is best  illustrated when discussing the  tournament of power during the show's  airing the consensus was that the show  was moving far too slowly and that there  was perhaps too much unneeded filler  however during every pivotal moment in  the tournament of power whether it be  the first universe's elimination the two  parts special Goku's fight with kale or  the ending that vocal portion of the fan  base that was complaining by and large  enjoyed what was transpiring on-screen  similarly many folks myself included  this time had serious problems with the  pacing of the Dragon Ball super manga  everything was happening far far too  quickly which as a result completely  ruined not only the endings sense of  suspense and tension but the entire arc  as a whole  in contrast the end to the animes  telling of the tournament of power was a  worldwide event the  three episodes had hundreds of thousands  of people over various parts of the  world watching the culmination of the  tournament unfold before their eyes why  because it was a spectacle there was a  sense of atmosphere tension and stakes  it paste itself correctly and earned  each and every moment it presented my  point is we put too much emphasis on  these monumental moments without  appreciating the time that's needed to  make these moments work as well as they  do  Super Saiyan Blue's unveiling was not  nearly as impactful as the original  Super Saiyan or even ultra instinct  because it never earned that moment  whereas the others did  Super Saiyan blue just showed up  expecting that to be enough and it just  wasn't the same  however despite everything when all is  said and done Dragon Ball will be  remembered for these moments these epic  larger-than-life moments snapshots in  time that will fill those that watch  them with nostalgia for the good old  days and so fans will always yearn for  more moments like these more and more  frequently not understanding or  appreciating that these in isolation are  not what makes Dragon Ball great it's  the story that makes these moments what  they are without the necessary context  these moments mean nothing but when give  it enough time and the right story they  excite us make us cheer laugh and cry so  when you're watching your favorite show  are looking back on your favorite  moments from a series try to keep in  mind appreciate and above all else  remember  the importance of waiting thanks for  watching  [Music]

